{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he shall say to them: Hear, O Israel.</b> Rashi explained: even if you have no merit other than the recitation of Shema alone, etc. — but is the recitation of Shema such a trivial matter? And it appears to me that the main thing concerns the tefillin on the head, for one who recites Shema presumably does not recite it except by means of tefillin, so that he not be like one testifying false testimony about himself (Berakhot 14a), and by means of the tefillin on his head terror and dread will fall upon the enemies, as it is written (Deuteronomy 28:10): ״וְרָאוּ כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ כִּי שֵׁם ה׳ נִקְרָא עָלֶיךָ וְיָרְאוּ מִמֶּךָּ״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 6a): these are the tefillin of the head. And for this matter they needed, in war, that the haters should see and fear them. And moreover the recitation of Shema of the night — just as it protects from every harmful and destructive thing, so too it protects from the nations whose power comes from the celestial princes above. And therefore they drew support (Bemidbar Rabbah 20:2) from the recitation of Shema to the verse ״לֹא יִשְׁכַּב עַד יֹאכַל טֶרֶף וְדַם חֲלָלִים יִשְׁתֶּה״ (Numbers 23:24), for its plain sense presumably speaks simply of war in general." "And in tractate Berakhot (5a) our Rabbis of blessed memory said: a person should always rouse his good impulse against the evil impulse, as it is said (Psalms 4:5): ״רִגְזוּ וְאַל תֶּחֱטָאוּ״. If it departs, fine. If not, let him engage in Torah, as it is said: ״אִמְרוּ בִלְבַבְכֶם״. If it departs, fine. And if not, let him recite Shema, as it is said: ״עַל מִשְׁכַּבְכֶם״. If it departs, fine. If not, let him remind himself of the day of death, as it is said: ״וְדֹמּוּ סֶלָה״. And these four are alluded to in this parashah, because it is said: ״כִּי תֵצֵא מַחֲנֶה עַל אוֹיְבֶיךָ וְנִשְׁמַרְתָּ מִכֹּל דָּבָר רָע״, which Rashi explained: because the Satan prosecutes at a time of danger. And this prosecution means that the Satan is the evil impulse, which strives to cause a person to sin in war, a place of danger, in order to deliver him into the hands of his enemies. Therefore in war a person needs more guarding from the evil impulse, for in this dangerous place it attacks a person more than in any other place, to turn him toward evil. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Ketuvot 46a) learned from the verse ״וְנִשְׁמַרְתָּ מִכֹּל דָּבָר רָע״ that a person should not fantasize by day and come to emission at night. But is it only in time of war that one must be careful and not at other times? Rather, certainly because in war the evil impulse attacks him more — whether in matters of forbidden relations or in other transgressions — in order to deliver him into the hands of his enemies, therefore the Torah taught them that they should be vigilant in war to stand against their impulse, so that it not bring them to sin in a place of danger. And know that in the Akeidah and among other commentators I saw that they explained all the wars in this parashah as referring to a person's wars with his impulse. But this is far from my understanding, to remove the verses from their plain sense. And what we have written accords with the plain sense from every side, to indicate the path by which the nations will prevail — namely, when they are whole in their deeds in war and not one man among them is found lacking through transgression." "Therefore it is said: ״וְהָיָה כְּקָרְבְכֶם אֶל הַמִּלְחָמָה״ against the nations, and you fear sin — that the evil impulse not cause you to stumble, for it is the Satan who prosecutes at a time of danger — then they shall engage in Torah. This is what it says: ״וְנִגַּשׁ הַכֹּהֵן וְדִבֶּר אֶל הָעָם״, and in tractate Sotah (42a) they said: ״וְדִבֶּר אֶל הָעָם״ — in the holy tongue, and we derive it from a gezerah shavah of ״מֹשֶׁה יְדַבֵּר״ stated at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 19:19): just as there it was in the holy tongue, so too here. And I say: there is no gezerah shavah for partial matters — just as there it was Torah study, so too here the priest speaks to the people words of Torah, for Joshua was punished for neglect of Torah study in war, as the angel said to him: ״עַתָּה בָּאתִי״ (Joshua 5:14) on account of neglecting Torah (Eruvin 63b). And the reason is that Torah study shields against the evil impulse, the Satan who prosecutes more intensely at a time of danger. And perhaps you will say: there is a person who is not a Torah scholar, for not everyone is learned in Torah, and if so, how will those ranks be saved from the hand of this inner enemy in war? Therefore, ״וְאָמַר שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that is, let him recite Shema, which everyone knows how to recite, and by this all of them will be saved from the mouth of the enemy. And perhaps you have an ignoramus who does not even know the recitation of Shema, or some stiff-necked man whose evil impulse is stronger than he is, who will not turn back on account of any Torah or recitation of Shema — then one must remind him of the day of death, and then it will certainly depart. Therefore the officer proclaims: ״מִי הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר בָּנָה בַיִת וְגוֹ׳ פֶּן יָמוּת בַּמִּלְחָמָה״ etc., and through the reminder of the death of war he will remember incidentally also the day of death in general, and by this he will surely conquer his impulse, as it is written (Genesis 4:7): ״אִם תֵּיטִיב שְׂאֵת וְאִם לֹא תֵּיטִיב לַפֶּתַח חַטָּאת רֹבֵץ״ — that is, let him remind himself of the opening of his grave, and then even though ״אֵלֶיךָ תְּשׁוּקָתוֹ״ from every direction, nevertheless ״אַתָּה תִּמְשָׁל בּוֹ״." "And on this our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Berakhot 18b): ״כִּי הַחַיִּים יוֹדְעִים שֶׁיָּמוּתוּ״ (Ecclesiastes 9:5) — these are the righteous, etc. — meaning that they know they will die, and therefore the remembrance of the day of death is always before their eyes, and this matter saves them from sin. ״וְהַמֵּתִים אֵינָם יוֹדְעִין מְאוּמָה״ — these are the wicked, who in their lifetime are called dead, as it is said (Ezekiel 21:30): ״וְאַתָּה חָלָל רָשָׁע וְגוֹ׳ אֲשֶׁר בָּא יוֹמוֹ בְּעֵת עֲוֹן קֵץ״ — that is, the wicked know nothing, for there is no remembrance of the day of death before their eyes at all. And this is what it says: ״אֲשֶׁר בָּא יוֹמוֹ בְּעֵת עֲוֹן קֵץ״ — meaning, on account of the sin of his end, for the remembrance of his end was not before his eyes at all — not like David, who prayed and said: ״הוֹדִיעֵנִי ה׳ קִצִּי״ etc. (Psalms 39:5), but rather ״קִרְבָּם בָּתֵּימוֹ לְעוֹלָם״ etc. (Psalms 49:12). And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The phrase אַטּוּ קְרִיאַת שְׁמַע מִלְּתָא זוּטַרְתִּי הִיא is an Aramaic challenge formula; rendered literally as 'but is the recitation of Shema such a trivial matter?'", 1 "The references to the Akeidah (עקדה) likely mean the Akeidat Yitzchak of R. Isaac Arama, a work of Torah commentary known for such allegorical readings.", 2 "The phrase מַה לְהַלָּן תַּלְמוּד תּוֹרָה אַף כָּאן is the author's own alternative to the gezerah shavah, using the formula style of Talmudic comparison.", 3 "In the Ezekiel citation the Kli Yakar reads חָלָל רָשָׁע following the verse in Ezekiel 21:30 (21:25 in some numbering); the phrase קִרְבָּם בָּתֵּימוֹ לְעוֹלָם is cited from Psalms 49:12, the word בָּתֵּימוֹ understood as 'their graves,' per Rabbinic interpretation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:23.837594979Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}